WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.240
From whale mouths to whale memories and Kiri’s&nbsp;
consequential connection – the Tsaheylu is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.500
full display in “Avatar: The Way of Water”&nbsp;
and it’s complicated beyond connection.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.020
When "Avatar" first came out in 2009, many&nbsp;
were quick to draw comparisons between it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.940
and "Pocahontas" or "Dances With Wolves."&nbsp;
The movie still managed to set itself apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.500
in terms of its spectacular world-building.&nbsp;
James Cameron and his team created an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.720
world and language filled with new animals and&nbsp;
customs, making the movie feel truly lived-in.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.940
The Na'vi felt like a real civilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.400
complete with religion based on something real&nbsp;
in the world of Pandora. The Na'vi worship Eywa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.660
which is based on the real neural connections&nbsp;
that all of the fauna and flora on the planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.040
remain connected to and in “Avatar: The Way&nbsp;
of Water” they aren’t as simple anymore.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.260
While in the first film, the Na'vi can tap into&nbsp;
this Eywa via the Tsaheylu, which requires them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.000
to connect their ponytails with other materials&nbsp;
to allow them to feel and communicate with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.320
the other plant or animal is feeling. However,&nbsp;
there's much more to learn about this ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.120
and "Avatar: The Way of Water" reveals more&nbsp;
ways the Na'vi can bond with those around them.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.820
“They will be introduced to a new oceanic clan&nbsp;
and an array of diverse aquatic creatures.”

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.100
Bonding plays an important part in "Avatar: The&nbsp;
Way of Water," as Jake Sully and Neytiri ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.140
mountain banshees to get around. Once the two take&nbsp;
their family to the Metkayina clan, they learn how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.180
to bond with ilus and skimwings, which basically&nbsp;
function as their forest-based counterparts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.220
allowing the Na'vi to travel with ease. However,&nbsp;
the main animal on display in "The Way of Water"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.020
is the tulkun, which is a giant whale-like&nbsp;
creature, but it has a very unique way of bonding.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.220
Instead of attaching to an outer-exposed&nbsp;
mechanism like in the first Avatar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.600
the Na'vi must enter the tulkun's mouth.&nbsp;
Only there will they find the neural point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.220
as we see with Lo'ak [LO-ack] and Payakan&nbsp;
– a young tulkun he befriends. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.160
Lo'ak doesn't use this&nbsp;
connection to control Payakan.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.600
Instead, he sees what Payakan saw on one&nbsp;
fateful day, which just so happens to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.840
the reason why the Metkayina and&nbsp;
other tulkuns want nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.620
with him. They view him as a killer,&nbsp;
which goes against the tulkun way.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.360
Up until this point, we hadn't seen that bonding&nbsp;
could lead to such a phenomenon. Lo'ak actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.100
watches Payakan's memories, allowing greater&nbsp;
depth and understanding within their bond.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.860
Kiri has her own unique experience with bonding&nbsp;
but with almost tragic consequences. Some kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.480
from the Metkayina clan take Kiri to a special&nbsp;
place in the reef where she can bond with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.740
unique structure. When she connects to it, she&nbsp;
receives an image in her head of her speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:34.020
to the human form of Dr. Grace Augustine.&nbsp;
However, things take a dark turn when Kiri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.400
begins seizing underwater, forcing her new&nbsp;
friends to take her to the surface for aid.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.180
It's unclear exactly why bonding caused&nbsp;
Kiri to have a seizure in this instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.380
although Dr. Norm Spellman and Dr.&nbsp;
Max Patel insist that it was a form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.380
of epilepsy with the bonding&nbsp;
acting as a kind of trigger.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.800
Sigourney Weaver, who plays Kiri in&nbsp;
the film, did say in an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.000
with Screen Rant that Kiri was more deeply&nbsp;
connected to nature than others around her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.080
so it's possible that had something to&nbsp;
do with the reaction. As she explained:

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.820
"There is a scene where she talks about hearing&nbsp;
Eywa's breathing. Eywa is [why] Kiri can hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.660
things that other people don't hear. I&nbsp;
think it makes Kiri quite uncomfortable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.940
but I think because she's so sensitive to all of&nbsp;
nature that she would also be sensitive to Eywa."

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.240
Clearly, there's more to bonding than&nbsp;
simply being able to ride an animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.900
around like a pony. With plenty&nbsp;
of sequels reportedly on deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.140
chances are good we'll learn more&nbsp;
about bonding in the years to come.

